Supreme People's Court

Specific Provisions on Jurisdiction of Foreign-Related Maritime Litigation

(Adopted by the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court on January 31, 1986)

For the purpose of safeguarding national sovereignty, protecting the lawful rights and interests of parties to maritime litigation, and facilitating the prompt conduct of maritime litigation, the following specific provisions concerning the jurisdiction of Chinese maritime courts over foreign-related maritime litigation are hereby formulated in accordance with the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China (for Trial Implementation), the Marine Environmental Protection Law of the People's Republic of China, the Maritime Traffic Safety Law of the People's Republic of China, and the relevant international conventions to which China is a party, as well as with reference to international customary practice:

I. Actions for claims arising from collision of vessels or damage caused by vessels to offshore operational facilities or other maritime accidents

1. Where the collision of vessels or other maritime accident occurs within Chinese ports, internal waters, territorial sea, or other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;

2. Where the collision of vessels or other maritime accident occurs outside the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction, but the first port of arrival of the damaged vessel or the offending vessel is a Chinese port, the offending vessel or other vessels belonging to the owner of the offending vessel are detained in a Chinese port, or the port of registry of the damaged vessel or the offending vessel is a Chinese port;

3. Where the collision of vessels or other maritime accident causes casualties or property damage to Chinese citizens.

II. Actions for claims arising from damage caused by offshore operations or improper installation of facilities impeding the safe navigation of vessels, personal injury occurring in the course of maritime transport, maritime operations, or port operations, or major liability accidents occurring in maritime transport or maritime operations

1. Where the accident occurs within the territorial sea of China or other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;

2. Where the first port of arrival of the damaged vessel is a Chinese port;

3. Where the accident causes casualties or property damage to Chinese citizens.

III. Actions for claims arising from pollution of sea areas caused by discharge of oil from vessels, dumping of waste or other harmful substances into the sea, or improper marine oil exploration and development or ship-breaking operations

1. Where the pollution damage occurs within the territorial sea of China or other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;

2. Where the pollution accident occurs outside the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction, but causes pollution to the sea areas of China;

3. Where China has adopted preventive measures to prevent or mitigate pollution damage.

IV. Actions instituted for the recovery of expenses for salvage or refloating operations

1. Where the salvage or refloating operations are carried out within the territorial sea of China or other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;

2. Where the first port of arrival of the salving vessel or the salved vessel is a Chinese port.

V. Actions instituted for disputes arising from marine development and comprehensive utilization contracts

1. Where the place of performance of the contract is within the territorial sea of China or other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;

2. Where the development and utilization involve the territorial sea of China or other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction.

VI. Actions instituted for disputes arising from towage contracts

1. Where the contract is concluded or performed in China;

2. Where the vessel of either or both parties to the contract arrives at a Chinese port or enters the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction.

VII. Actions instituted for disputes arising from contracts for carriage of goods by sea

1. Where the contract is concluded or performed in China;

2. Where the port of loading, port of discharge, port of destination, port of call of the vessel, or the port of departure, port of transshipment, port of arrival or port of destination for passengers and their baggage are Chinese ports;

3. Where the contract or bill of lading contains a clause stipulating that the dispute shall be subject to the jurisdiction of Chinese courts.

VIII. Actions instituted for disputes arising from time charter party or bareboat charter party

1. Where the contract is concluded or performed in China;

2. Where the port of registry of the vessel or the place of redelivery of the vessel is a Chinese port.

IX. Actions instituted for disputes arising from ship agency contracts

1. Where the contract is concluded or performed in China;

2. Where the business establishment of either party to the contract is located within the territory of China;

3. Where the principal's vessel enters the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction or arrives at a Chinese port.

X. Actions instituted for disputes arising from port loading and unloading operations or tallying, where the loading, unloading operations, or tallying are carried out in a Chinese port.

XI. Actions instituted for disputes arising from ship repair contracts

1. Where the contract is concluded in China or the vessel is repaired in China;

2. Where a vessel repaired abroad arrives at a Chinese port or enters the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction.

XII. Actions instituted for disputes arising from marine insurance contracts

1. Where the principal place of business or permanent establishment of the insurer is located within the territory of China;

2. Where the insured vessel or cargo arrives at a Chinese port.

XIII. Actions instituted for general average

1. Where the general average act occurs within the territorial sea of China or other sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;

2. Where the general average adjustment is carried out in China;

3. Where the port of termination of the voyage of the vessel involved in the general average is a Chinese port.

XIV. Actions instituted for disputes arising from shipbuilding or ship sale and purchase contracts

1. Where the contract is concluded or performed in China;

2. Where the vessel built or purchased arrives at a Chinese port or enters the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction.

XV. Actions instituted for disputes arising from mortgage of a vessel or maritime lien

1. Where the mortgage or maritime lien is created or arises within China;

2. Where the vessel arrives at a Chinese port or enters the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction.

XVI. Actions instituted for disputes arising from labor service contracts between crew members and shipowners or employers

1. Where the contract is concluded or performed in China;

2. Where the vessel on which the crew member serves arrives at a Chinese port or enters the sea areas under Chinese jurisdiction;

3. Where the crew member, shipowner, or employer is a Chinese citizen.

XVII. In addition to the above provisions, Chinese maritime courts shall also have jurisdiction over any foreign-related maritime litigation falling under any of the following circumstances:

1. Where the defendant has a domicile, habitual residence, principal place of business, or permanent establishment within the territory of China;

2. Where a Chinese maritime court, upon application by a maritime claimant to preserve the exercise of the maritime claim, has already arrested a vessel, or the parties have already provided security within China;

3. Where the defendant has other property within the territory of China that may be subject to arrest;

4. Where both parties have agreed by agreement to institute proceedings in a Chinese court.
